---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import MonarchDemo from '../../components/MonarchDemo';
import '../../styles/v9.css';
import '../../styles/monarch.css';
const measurements=[
 {label:'MIX_M40',context:'192',environment:'Chrome · fullscreen',tps:'1,574.4',ms:'0.6352',ci:'0.6319–0.6381',width:100,selected:true},
 {label:'N80 control',context:'192',environment:'Chrome · fullscreen',tps:'1,391.8',ms:'0.7185',ci:'0.7145–0.7234',width:88.4},
 {label:'MIX_M40',context:'1,024',environment:'Chrome · fullscreen',tps:'1,433.2',ms:'0.6977',ci:'0.6928–0.7005',width:91.0},
 {label:'MIX_M40',context:'192',environment:'Chrome · windowed',tps:'1,385.7',ms:'0.7216',ci:'0.7156–0.7262',width:88.0},
];
---
<Layout title="MONARCH — 1,574 tokens/s in a browser · Inductive ML" description="How we optimized LFM2.5-230M WebGPU kernels, measured 1,574 tokens/s on an M4 Max, and tested the limits of a persistent decoder. Try the real model locally." analytics={false}>
 <div class="page monarch-page">
  <header class="masthead">
   <canvas id="lava" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
   <div class="grain"></div>
   <div class="masthead-inner">
    <div class="mh-top">
     <a class="mh-back" href="/experiments">← Experiments</a>
     <nav class="top-nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/">Inductive ML</a><a href="/about">About</a><a href="mailto:aditya@inductive.ml">Contact</a></nav>
    </div>
    <div><div class="page-kicker">Research · MONARCH · 2026</div><h1 class="page-title">Faster Tokens.<br/>Same Small Model.</h1></div>
   </div>
  </header>
  <main class="v9 monarch-research">
   <div class="pad"><div class="grid12">
    <div class="secbar"><span class="num">§ 00</span><span class="ttl">// research-explainer</span><span class="meta">September 2026 · WebGPU</span></div>
    <div class="paper-hero">
     <div class="head"><p class="sub">How far can careful GPU programming take a language model in a browser? <em>We optimized the kernels, kept the model fixed, and measured the limits.</em></p><div class="links"><a class="btn accent" href="#try-it">Try it on your GPU →</a><a class="btn" href="#method">Read the experiment ↓</a><a class="btn" href="/monarch/research-results.json" download>Recorded results ↗</a></div></div>
     <div class="meta-rail"><div class="row"><span>// code</span><span>MONARCH</span></div><div class="row"><span>// status</span><span>experimental</span></div><div class="row"><span>// model</span><span>LFM2.5 · 230M</span></div><div class="row"><span>// weights</span><span>int4 / block 32</span></div><div class="row"><span>// stack</span><span>WebGPU · WGSL</span></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout overview-callout"><span class="lbl">// tl;dr</span><p>The same small model, with a faster path for every token. We measured <strong>1,574.4 tokens/s</strong> in fullscreen Chrome on an M4 Max. Try the real decoder below and measure your own GPU.</p></div>
    <div class="stat-strip monarch-stats"><div class="stat"><span class="idx">// recorded · 192 context</span><span class="val accent">1,574.4 <small>tok/s</small></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="idx">// recorded · 1,024 context</span><span class="val">1,433.2 <small>tok/s</small></span></div><div class="stat"><span class="idx">// dispatches per token</span><span class="val">80 → 72</span></div></div>
    <div class="secbar" id="try-it"><span class="num">§ 01</span><span class="ttl">// hands-on demo</span><span class="meta">your browser · your GPU</span></div>
    <div class="paper"><p>Load the real model. Choose a task or write your own prompt, then measure your tokens per second. Generation runs locally on your GPU.</p></div>
    <div class="demo-section"><MonarchDemo client:only="react"><div slot="fallback" class="demo-placeholder">Preparing the on-device demo…</div></MonarchDemo><noscript><p class="measurement-note">Enable JavaScript to run the on-device demo. The experiment and recorded measurements remain available below.</p></noscript><p class="under-demo">The lab figures above are recorded results. The demo starts empty and reports your actual run. Its four-run benchmark is a quick local measurement; the full lab protocol is described below.</p></div>
<div class="secbar" id="question"><span class="num">§ 02</span><span class="ttl">// the question</span><span class="meta">single-stream decode</span></div>
<section class="paper monarch-article" aria-label="the question"><h2>The model fits.<br/>Can the math move faster?</h2><p class="article-lead">A small language model can fit on a laptop GPU. Getting it to generate quickly is a different problem.</p><p>Every new token still needs matrix multiplications, normalization, attention, cache updates, and a vocabulary projection. At this scale, moving weights and coordinating many small GPU programs can cost as much as their arithmetic.</p><p>MONARCH is our series of experiments on the decode path of <a href="https://huggingface.co/LiquidAI/LFM2.5-230M" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Liquid AI’s LFM2.5-230M</a>. We kept the model fixed and worked on the WGSL programs that execute it. There was no training. The target was 1,500–2,000 tokens per second for one sequence on an Apple M4 Max.</p><div class="callout monarch-callout"><span>THE RESULT</span><p>We measured <strong>1,574.4 tok/s</strong> in fullscreen Chrome. The 2,000 tok/s target did not pass. The selected kernel remains experimental.</p></div></section>
<div class="secbar" id="method"><span class="num">§ 03</span><span class="ttl">// the work</span><span class="meta">kernel efficiency</span></div>
<section class="paper monarch-article" aria-label="the work"><h2>Optimize the actual<br/>path of a token.</h2><p>The selected configuration, <code>MIX_M40</code>, combines several changes. We screened variants, checked them against pinned outputs, and compared complete decode runs with an interleaved control.</p>
     <div class="kernel-flow" aria-label="Simplified path of a generated token"><span>Token</span><b>→</b><span>8 conv + 6 attention layers<br/><small>with feed-forward blocks</small></span><b>→</b><span>Vocabulary<br/>+ next token</span></div>
     <ol class="method-list"><li><span>01</span><div><h3>Fuse useful work</h3><p>The convolution input projection and its three-tap core share one kernel. This helps reduce the control’s 80 dispatches to <strong>72 per token</strong>. A complete token stays in one compute pass.</p></div></li><li><span>02</span><div><h3>Match the geometry to the matrix</h3><p>Different projections use different workgroup sizes and rows per subgroup. The chosen feed-forward kernel uses 40 slices and 1,024 threads, pairs the gate and up projections, and finishes with an ordered reduction.</p></div></li><li><span>03</span><div><h3>Keep attention and reductions local</h3><p>Online subgroup attention uses 32 position splits. Subgroup-assisted normalization and folding reduce repeated work. The fold assigns 16 lanes to a row.</p></div></li><li><span>04</span><div><h3>Make precision explicit</h3><p>Weights remain int4. Hidden activations and caches use f16; residuals and logits use f32. Selected short dot products use f16, with f32 accumulation between them. Precision changes must pass the same correctness checks as geometry changes.</p></div></li></ol>
     <h3 class="subheading">Correctness before the stopwatch</h3><p>We compared top-1 outputs, sampled full-vocabulary logit rows, inspected caches, and repeated deterministic continuations. The selected arm’s top-1 agreement was 100% at context 192 and 99.951% at 1,024, with three-run determinism checks passing.</p><p>For the native-browser confirmation, we used 16 blocks of 64 generated tokens per arm and context, discarded 64 warmup tokens, and interleaved the N80 control in rotating, reversed order. We recorded whole-pass wall time, confidence intervals, visibility, and thermal drift. Fullscreen and windowed runs were separate conditions. The research Chromium launch enabled experimental WebGPU APIs and disabled timestamp quantization; the public demo uses your browser’s normal configuration.</p>
    </section>
<div class="secbar" id="results"><span class="num">§ 04</span><span class="ttl">// the numbers</span><span class="meta">M4 Max · recorded</span></div>
<section class="paper monarch-article" aria-label="the numbers"><h2>Fast is a measurement,<br/>with conditions attached.</h2><p>These are recorded M4 Max measurements from September 6, 2026. Prompt prefill and model loading are excluded from decode throughput.</p>
     <div class="throughput-plot" aria-label="Recorded throughput comparison">{measurements.map(r=><div class="throughput-row"><div><strong>{r.label}</strong><span>{r.context} context · {r.environment.includes('windowed')?'windowed':'fullscreen'}</span></div><div class="throughput-track"><span style={`width:${r.width}%`} class={r.selected?'selected':''}/></div><b>{r.tps}<small>tok/s</small></b></div>)}</div>
     <div class="table-scroll"><table class="v9-table"><caption>Recorded wall time per token · 95% bootstrap confidence interval</caption><thead><tr class="head"><th>Kernel / context</th><th>Browser mode</th><th>ms/token</th><th>95% CI, ms</th></tr></thead><tbody>{measurements.map(r=><tr><td>{r.label} / {r.context}</td><td>{r.environment.replace('Chrome · ','')}</td><td>{r.ms}</td><td>{r.ci}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
     <p>MIX_M40’s paired speedup over the N80 control was <strong>1.136×</strong> at context 192 and <strong>1.2075×</strong> at 1,024. Correctness and thermal checks passed. Whole-token throughput cleared the fullscreen 1,500 tok/s target.</p><p>The inherited component-attribution check did not pass: summed per-kernel timing did not reconcile closely enough with the whole-pass timing. The recorded status is <code>NO_VERDICT_SPLIT</code>. We publish the observed throughput while keeping adoption separate.</p>
    </section>
<div class="secbar" id="visibility"><span class="num">§ 05</span><span class="ttl">// the next limit</span><span class="meta">memory visibility</span></div>
<section class="paper monarch-article" aria-label="the next limit"><h2>Could one big kernel<br/>replace the whole chain?</h2><p>A persistent decoder might remove dispatch boundaries and overlap work between tasks. First, it needs a reliable way for one workgroup to publish data to another inside the same dispatch. We tested that memory contract before building a persistent token kernel.</p><div class="visibility-comparison"><div><span>PLAIN PAYLOAD LOADS</span><strong>Failed</strong><p>Ordinary, fresh, flag-dependent, and combined addressing all produced stale words.</p></div><div><span>ATOMIC STORES + LOADS</span><strong>100,000 / 100,000</strong><p>Handoffs passed. A separate 100,000-handoff chain passed with zero timeouts or device losses.</p></div></div>
     <p>The resident test used <strong>200 workgroups × 512 threads</strong>, 16 KiB of workgroup storage, and a 4 KiB payload. The result was empirical: an all-atomic pattern worked on this stack. It did not distinguish compiler hoisting from cache staleness, and it did not establish portable cross-device guarantees.</p><h3 class="subheading">Then we priced the cost</h3><p>We retained 72 separate dispatches and forced all task kinds through a common entry point. This measured the cost of shared geometry and resource mapping without building a persistent decoder. A second version added atomic activation reads, stores, and f16 packing.</p>
     <div class="table-scroll"><table class="v9-table"><caption>B1.11a · observed WG512 pricing · ms/token</caption><thead><tr class="head"><th>Term</th><th>192 context</th><th>1,024 context</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Common-kernel tax</td><td>+0.657</td><td>+1.525</td></tr><tr><td>Additional atomic contract tax</td><td>+0.101</td><td>+0.166</td></tr><tr><td>Modeled net gain</td><td>−0.718</td><td>−1.607</td></tr><tr><td>With maximal overlap allowance</td><td>−0.627</td><td>−1.380</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
     <p>At context 192, forced geometry alone cost 0.026 ms. The common entry point and its resource mapping accounted for most of the larger cost. Read-only atomic bandwidth had looked nearly free in isolation; the integrated model also paid for stores and packing.</p><div class="callout monarch-callout muted-callout"><span>WHAT THIS DOES — AND DOES NOT — ESTABLISH</span><p><strong>96/96 correctness cases passed.</strong> The measured costs made the model’s predicted gain negative. But background GPU activity and timing drift failed the clean-run conditions, so the formal price remained unadjudicated. No persistent token kernel was built or timed.</p></div><p>The live demo above uses the working, ordinary-dispatch MIX_M40 decoder. It does not use the experimental cross-workgroup memory protocol.</p>
    </section>
<div class="secbar" id="compatibility"><span class="num">§ 06</span><span class="ttl">// run it yourself</span><span class="meta">device requirements</span></div>
<section class="paper monarch-article" aria-label="run it yourself"><h2>Same code.<br/>Your own result.</h2><p>The demo checks your adapter before downloading the model. This version needs WebGPU, <code>shader-f16</code>, fixed 32-lane <code>subgroups</code>, 1,024-thread workgroups, and a storage binding large enough for the model. Recent Chromium on a compatible Apple Silicon Mac is the tested path.</p><p>An unsupported browser gets a specific explanation. This is especially relevant on iPhones and browsers that do not expose the required shader features. There is no simulated TPS counter or silent fallback to a server.</p><p>The first load downloads approximately 174 MB of weights and tokenizer data. Verified weight chunks can be cached locally. Prompts and generated text stay in the browser. The demo uses greedy decoding and a shorter context limit than the full model; longer prompts and streaming readback affect the measured speed.</p><p>To compare runs, use AC power, keep the tab visible, pause other GPU-heavy apps, and repeat after the machine cools. The four-run demo is a quick measurement; reproducing the lab claim requires the full protocol and the same browser mode.</p><a href="#try-it" class="text-link">Back to the live demo ↑</a></section>
<div class="secbar" id="evidence"><span class="num">§ 07</span><span class="ttl">// evidence & credits</span><span class="meta">open notes</span></div>
<section class="paper monarch-article" aria-label="evidence & credits"><h2>Follow the evidence.</h2><div class="evidence-links"><a href="/monarch/research-results.json" download><span>Recorded results & gate status<small>B1.8 + B1.11a · JSON</small></span><b>↓</b></a><a href="/monarch/source-manifest.json" download><span>Decoder source manifest<small>Pinned source hashes · unchanged kernel bodies</small></span><b>↓</b></a><a href="/monarch/model-manifest.json"><span>Model layout & checksum<small>Canonical weights ca1fea89…</small></span><b>↗</b></a><a href="https://huggingface.co/LiquidAI/LFM2.5-230M" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><span>Liquid AI model card<small>LFM2.5-230M · model architecture and usage</small></span><b>↗</b></a><a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/webml-community/lfm2-webgpu-kernels" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><span>The reference browser demo<small>webml-community · inspiration for this hands-on interface</small></span><b>↗</b></a></div><p class="credits">Model by Liquid AI. Kernel experiments and this demo by Inductive ML. Tokenization uses Hugging Face tokenizers.js. <a href="/monarch/LICENSE-LFM.txt">Model license</a> · <a href="/monarch/NOTICE.txt">Attribution & modifications</a>. Research records: September 6–11, 2026.</p></section>
    <footer class="foot"><div class="a">Inductive ML</div><div class="b">MONARCH · September 2026</div><div class="c"><a href="/experiments">All experiments ↗</a></div></footer>
   </div></div>
  </main>
 </div>
</Layout>
<script>
 import {initLava} from '../../lib/lava';
 const canvas=document.getElementById('lava');
 if(canvas instanceof HTMLCanvasElement){
  // Match the shared masthead without an animation competing with inference.
  const lava=initLava(canvas,{scale:2.6,speed:0.7,dim:0.9,animate:false});
  window.addEventListener('pagehide',()=>lava?.destroy(),{once:true});
 }
</script>
